### How body content splits into first/second half
Compiled HTML is split at the `<h2>` closest to the midpoint of all `<h2>`
blocks in the post — NOT an exact 50/50 split, NOT /blog's 33/34/33
pattern. A post needs **at least 2 `<h2>` headings** for a second half to
exist at all; with 0-1 headings, everything renders as one block and the
mid-content newsletter/poll/social section is skipped entirely (nothing to
break at). This directly affects whether PollWidget can ever show up —
even with `post.poll` set, it won't render if the post has fewer than 2
`<h2>`s, because it's nested inside the `{secondHalf && (...)}` block.
**This is worth flagging to whoever writes posts: always use 2+ `<h2>`
sections, both for TOC usefulness and so the poll/mid-content block
actually appears.**

## WIDGET SYSTEM — HOW IT WORKS END TO END

### Architecture decision: Apps Script, NOT a database, NOT direct Sheets API

Two paths were considered. Direct Google Sheets API access via a service
account was built FIRST, then explicitly abandoned when the user asked
"why not Apps Script" — Apps Script is simpler (no GCP console, no service
account JSON key, no `googleapis` npm package needed) and fits how the
user already works with Sheets elsewhere in this project. **The
`googleapis` npm package was installed by the user but is no longer
needed** — dead weight, not harmful, just flagged for cleanup awareness.

The abandoned direct-Sheets-API file (`src/lib/nailfeedSheets.ts`) was
DELETED — if you see any reference to it anywhere (there shouldn't be,
verified clean via grep), that's stale.

### Why one row per event, not an incrementing cell
Google Sheets has no atomic increment. Two simultaneous "+1" writes to the
same cell can race and lose a vote. Design instead: every reaction/vote/
quiz-completion is its OWN ROW (append-only), and counts are computed by
reading + filtering rows on demand. The Apps Script backend ALSO uses
`LockService` around each write as a second layer of safety (belt and
suspenders — the row-per-event pattern avoids most races on its own,
locking prevents the remaining "two people click submit in the exact same
instant" edge case from creating duplicate dedup-check false negatives).

### Dedup — real, cookie-based, cross-visit
User explicitly required this (initial version was in-memory/session-only,
meaning a page refresh let someone vote again — user said this was not
acceptable). Fix: `nf_uid` cookie (1yr, httpOnly), sent with every widget
request. Server checks `(postSlug, widgetId, sessionId)` combination
against existing rows before writing — if a match exists, the write is
skipped and current counts are returned with `alreadySubmitted: true`.
**Quiz dedup is intentionally SOFTER than reaction/poll**: retaking a quiz
is a reasonable thing to want to do, so QuizWidget does NOT block
re-submission client-side — it just avoids double-counting the exact same
result from a rapid double-click server-side.

### Sheet schema (3 tabs, exact names matter)
```
NailFeed_Reactions:   timestamp | post_slug | reaction | session_id
NailFeed_PollVotes:   timestamp | post_slug | poll_id | option_id | session_id
NailFeed_QuizResults: timestamp | post_slug | quiz_id | answers | result_id | session_id
```
`answers` in QuizResults is a JSON-stringified array of chosen option ids,
e.g. `["q1_brunch","q2_soft"]` — stored as a string, not exploded into
columns, so the schema doesn't need to change if a quiz's question count
changes.

### Quiz scoring logic
Each answer option carries `weights: Record<resultId, number>`. On the
final question, `computeResult()` sums weights across all chosen answers
per result id, and the highest-scoring result id wins (first result in
`config.results` is the tiebreak default if scores are equal). This was
VERIFIED via a standalone Node test with a 2-question/2-result mock —
clean wins, mixed-answer tiebreaking, and equal-weight ties all resolved
correctly. The test post's real quiz frontmatter was also validated: all
weight keys in every question's options correctly map to a real result id
in the `results` array (checked via script, zero mismatches).

